## 📌 Overview

This project is an automated, event-driven **ETL (Extract, Transform, Load)** pipeline built entirely on AWS serverless services.

It reads raw, **nested JSON order data** from Amazon S3, **flattens** the customer and product information with Python, Pandas, and Boto3, converts the result to **Parquet**, and writes it back to S3. An **AWS Glue Crawler** then discovers the schema and registers it in the **Glue Data Catalog**, so the data can be queried instantly with **Amazon Athena** using standard SQL.

### ✨ Highlights

- 🚀 **Fully serverless:** no servers to provision or manage
- 🔄 **Event-driven:** an S3 `ObjectCreated` trigger can run the pipeline automatically
- 🧩 **Nested JSON → flat tables:** order, customer, and product data in one structured dataset
- 📦 **Columnar storage:** Parquet for smaller files and faster analytical queries
- 🔍 **SQL-ready:** schema auto-discovered and queryable through Athena
- 📊 **Observable:** Lambda logs and metrics captured in CloudWatch

---

## 🏗 Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[📄 Raw Nested JSON] --> B[(Amazon S3<br/>incoming/)]
    B -->|ObjectCreated trigger| C[⚡ AWS Lambda<br/>Python · Pandas · Boto3]
    C --> D[🔧 Transformation<br/>Flatten nested JSON]
    D --> E[📦 Parquet Conversion]
    E --> F[(Amazon S3<br/>Parquet output)]
    F --> G[🕷 AWS Glue Crawler]
    G --> H[📚 Glue Data Catalog]
    H --> I[🔎 Amazon Athena<br/>SQL Queries]
    C -.logs & metrics.-> J[📈 CloudWatch]
```

---

## 🛠 Technologies Used

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Python** | Data processing and transformation |
| **Pandas** | Flattening and structuring JSON records |
| **Boto3** | AWS service integration |
| **Amazon S3** | Raw and transformed data storage |
| **AWS Lambda** | Serverless ETL execution |
| **AWS Glue Crawler** | Automatic schema discovery |
| **AWS Glue Data Catalog** | Metadata management |
| **Amazon Athena** | SQL-based data querying |
| **Parquet** | Columnar storage format |
| **Amazon CloudWatch** | Lambda execution logs and monitoring |

---

## 🔁 Project Workflow

| Step | Stage | Description |
|:---:|---|---|
| 1 | **Data Ingestion** | Upload raw nested JSON order data to the S3 incoming folder. |
| 2 | **Lambda Execution** | Lambda reads the JSON object from S3 using Boto3. An S3 `ObjectCreated` notification can trigger it automatically once configured. |
| 3 | **Data Transformation** | Python and Pandas flatten nested order, customer, and product information into structured tabular records. |
| 4 | **Data Conversion** | The transformed dataset is converted from JSON-based input into Parquet format. |
| 5 | **Data Storage** | Processed Parquet files are stored in a separate S3 output folder. |
| 6 | **Schema Discovery** | The Glue Crawler scans the output location, discovers the schema, and updates the Glue Data Catalog. |
| 7 | **Data Analytics** | Athena runs SQL queries on the cataloged Parquet table. |
| 8 | **Monitoring** | CloudWatch captures Lambda execution logs and runtime metrics. |

---

## 🔧 Data Transformation

The pipeline extracts and structures the following fields:

| Category | Fields |
|---|---|
| 🧾 **Order** | Order ID, Order Date, Total Order Amount |
| 👤 **Customer** | Customer ID, Name, Email, Address |
| 📦 **Product** | Product ID, Product Name, Category, Price, Quantity |

---

## 🔎 Sample SQL Query

```sql
SELECT *
FROM "AwsDataCatalog"."etl_pipeline"."orders_parquet_datalake";
```

Once the table is cataloged, you can run further analytical queries to explore orders, customers, products, and transaction amounts.

